---
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import { NewsletterSignup } from '../../components/NewsletterSignup';

// Simple blog post metadata
const posts = [
  {
    slug: 'california-ab-1856-open-source-exemption',
    title: 'California Just Declared Open Source a Different Kind of Software. Unanimously.',
    description: 'AB 1856 passed the California Senate 39-0 and the Assembly 69-0, exempting GPL, MIT, BSD, and Apache software from age-verification mandates. Colorado did it too. Legislatures are learning what public-code advocates have always known: open source isn\'t just software, it\'s a different legal category.',
    date: '2026-08-29',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-ab-1856.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'The California bear flag beside a giant 39-0 vote tally and the words open source is different'
  },
  {
    slug: 'va-oracle-ehr-meltdown',
    title: 'From $10 Billion to $27 Billion: The VA\'s Oracle Disaster Is the Case for Public Code',
    description: 'On August 20, the VA added $17 billion to its Oracle health records contract. Seventeen of 170 hospitals are live. GAO says 12 priority fixes are unimplemented. This is what closed, proprietary, single-vendor government IT produces.',
    date: '2026-08-28',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-va-oracle.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A VA hospital beside a burning pile of contract papers with the figures $10B to $27B and 17 of 170 sites live'
  },
  {
    slug: 'npm-supply-chain-worm-wave',
    title: '400 Packages Poisoned in a Day: The npm Worm Wave',
    description: 'On August 4, the keyv worm hit 400+ npm packages with valid cryptographic signatures. Three weeks earlier, @asyncapi fell the same way. The year\'s supply-chain attacks keep proving the same point: signatures verify identity, not intent — and the commons needs public investment, not just attestations.',
    date: '2026-08-05',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-npm-worms.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A worm eating through a stack of package boxes with the words 400 packages poisoned and trusted signatures did not stop it'
  },
  {
    slug: 'maintainer-burnout-crisis',
    title: 'The Maintainers Are Quitting. Who Keeps the Lights On?',
    description: 'Jellyfin\'s project leader just stepped down from burnout. curl ended its bug bounty over AI slop. Kubernetes retired Ingress NGINX for lack of maintainers. The commons that governments want to depend on is maintained by exhausted volunteers — and 60% of them aren\'t paid.',
    date: '2026-07-20',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-burnout.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'An exhausted maintainer at a desk under a dim lamp with the words the maintainers are quitting and 60 percent unpaid'
  },
  {
    slug: 'open-weights-are-not-open-source',
    title: 'Open Weights Are Not Open Source. The AI Industry Hopes You Won\'t Notice.',
    description: 'In September 2026, the fight over what counts as \'open\' AI reached a boiling point. The stakes: whether governments buying AI get something they can audit — or just something they can download.',
    date: '2026-09-15',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-open-weights.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'Split comparison: downloadable model weights labeled open distribution versus fully open source AI with open code, data, and weights'
  },
  {
    slug: 'eu-public-procurement-act',
    title: 'Europe Can Now Prefer European Software. America Can\'t Even Prefer Open Software.',
    description: 'The EU\'s proposed Public Procurement Act writes \'European preference\' into public buying for the first time. Combined with the open source strategy, it stacks the deck against US proprietary vendors — and shows what procurement power looks like.',
    date: '2026-09-09',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-eu-procurement.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A gavel striking beside the words European preference with a 30 percent quality-over-price badge'
  },
  {
    slug: 'schleswig-holstein-80-percent',
    title: 'Schleswig-Holstein Hit 80%. The Migration Paid for Itself in Eight Months.',
    description: 'An update to our April story: Germany\'s open source migration crossed 80% of workstations, finished a 44,000-mailbox email move with zero data loss, and the payback math is now undeniable. France, Denmark, and China are joining the wave.',
    date: '2026-09-01',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-schleswig-80.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A progress bar at 80 percent beside the words 30,000 workstations freed and payback in eight months'
  },
  {
    slug: 'eu-2bn-open-source-strategy',
    title: 'Europe Just Bet €2 Billion on Public Code. America Is Still Writing Blank Checks.',
    description: 'The EU\'s Tech Sovereignty Package puts \'public money, public code\' and \'open source first\' procurement into official strategy. Here\'s why Brussels did it — and what it means for American tech giants.',
    date: '2026-06-03',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-eu-strategy.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A circle of EU stars merged with code brackets beside a giant €2 BILLION figure and the words public money, public code'
  },
  {
    slug: 'omb-ai-open-weights-mandate',
    title: 'Washington Ordered Its AI Open. The Inventory Says Almost Nobody Listened.',
    description: 'OMB Memorandum M-25-21 requires agencies to release custom AI code — including model weights — as open source. We dug through the 2026 federal AI use-case inventory: 3,611 use cases, 71 agency-trained models, and exactly one public weight release.',
    date: '2026-04-14',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-omb-ai.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'The US Capitol beside a locked model box with the figures 3,611 AI use cases and 1 open weight release'
  },
  {
    slug: 'grok-open-washing',
    title: '\'Open Source\' Theater: Musk Released Grok\'s Weights and Kept the Keys',
    description: 'Grok 2.5\'s \'open source\' release came with a license banning you from training competing models — and a termination clause for suing. That\'s not open source. It\'s marketing with a EULA.',
    date: '2026-02-10',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '5 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-grok.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A theater stage curtain pulled back to reveal a locked model box labeled open source with strings attached'
  },
  {
    slug: 'llama-is-not-open-source',
    title: 'Meta\'s Llama Isn\'t Open Source. Llama 4 Won\'t Even Run in Europe.',
    description: 'The world\'s most-used \'open\' AI model fails the open source definition — and its newest license geo-fences an entire continent. This is what happens when public infrastructure depends on a vendor\'s marketing department.',
    date: '2025-04-05',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-llama.jpg',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A model box stamped \'open\' behind a geo-fence blocking off Europe, with a padlock on the license'
  },
  {
    slug: 'welcome-to-public-code-us',
    title: 'Welcome to Public Code US',
    description: 'Why we\'re fighting for public code, and why you should care',
    date: '2026-03-16',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '5 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-welcome.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'The pandemic unemployment duplication problem: states each built the same system from scratch, spending hundreds of millions per state'
  },
  {
    slug: 'contact-your-representative',
    title: 'We Made It Easy to Contact Your Senator or Representative',
    description: 'You shouldn\'t need a lobbyist to talk to your elected officials. Here\'s how Public Code US puts direct democracy back in your hands.',
    date: '2026-03-27',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-contact-rep.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'Four steps to contact your representative directly'
  },
  {
    slug: 'the-black-box-contracting-problem',
    title: 'The $755 Billion Black Box: Why You Can\'t Know What Deloitte Built With Your Money',
    description: 'Federal agencies spend $755 billion a year on contracts — and more than a trillion dollars in spending can\'t be traced. Here\'s the sunlight problem at the heart of government IT.',
    date: '2026-04-03',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-black-box.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A padlock over the US Capitol dome surrounded by dollar signs representing opaque government contracts'
  },
  {
    slug: 'open-source-is-worth-8-trillion',
    title: 'Open Source Is Worth $8.8 Trillion — And We\'re Leaving Most of It on the Table',
    description: 'Harvard researchers just put a number on open source software\'s economic value: $8.8 trillion. The federal government spends over $100 billion a year on IT contracts. The math isn\'t complicated.',
    date: '2026-04-03',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-trillion.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A graph showing the $8.8 trillion demand-side value of open source software compared to what companies would spend without it'
  },
  {
    slug: 'germany-saved-17-million-america-should-too',
    title: 'Germany Is Saving $17 Million a Year by Ditching Microsoft. America Is Still Writing Blank Checks.',
    description: 'Schleswig-Holstein just migrated 30,000 government workers to open source and saved €15 million annually. France built a sovereign digital stack. Munich did it in 2003. What\'s America waiting for?',
    date: '2026-04-03',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-germany.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'Side-by-side comparison of European open source government savings versus US proprietary software spending'
  },
  {
    slug: 'the-code-that-decides-your-life',
    title: 'The Code That Decides Your Life Runs on Trade Secrets',
    description: 'Government software denies your benefits, sets your bail, flags your taxes, and scores your immigration case. You can\'t read it. You can\'t challenge it. You don\'t even know what it says about you.',
    date: '2026-04-21',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-civil-liberties.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A person standing before a courtroom screen displaying an algorithm score with no explanation — \'RISK: HIGH. REASON: PROPRIETARY.\''
  },
  {
    slug: 'the-vendor-lock-in-tax',
    title: 'You\'re Paying the Vendor Lock-In Tax. Every Single Year.',
    description: 'Microsoft, Oracle, and SAP have turned government software into a permanent subscription. The Netherlands is fighting back. Denmark quit Office 365. America is still writing the checks.',
    date: '2026-05-04',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '8 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-lock-in.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'A padlock on a government building door labeled: Microsoft Enterprise Agreement — Renew or lose access to Azure'
  },
  {
    slug: 'estonia-digital-government-open-source',
    title: 'A Country of 1.3 Million People Solved Digital Government. On Open Source. Twenty Years Ago.',
    description: 'Estonia files taxes in five minutes, votes online, and lets citizens see exactly who accessed their records. The secret isn\'t money or technology. It\'s a choice about who owns the infrastructure.',
    date: '2026-05-11',
    author: 'The Public Code US Team',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    featuredImage: '/feature-estonia.png',
    featuredImageAlt: 'Estonia\'s X-Road open source data exchange layer connecting government databases versus the US patchwork of siloed proprietary systems'
  }
];

// Sort by date, newest first
const sortedPosts = posts.sort((a, b) => new Date(b.date).getTime() - new Date(a.date).getTime());
---

<Layout
  title="Blog - Public Code US Campaign Updates"
  description="Latest news, updates, and stories from the Public Code US campaign"
  currentPage="/blog"
>
  {/* Hero */}
  <section class="bg-am-light-blue text-am-light border-b-4 border-am-dark py-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4">
      <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-bold mb-6">Campaign Blog</h1>
      <p class="text-lg font-bold">
        Stories, updates, and insights from the Public Code US movement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Blog Posts */}
  <section class="bg-am-light border-b-4 border-am-dark py-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="space-y-8">
        {sortedPosts.map((post) => (
          <article>
            <a
              href={`/blog/${post.slug}`}
              class="border-4 border-am-dark block hover:shadow-am-lg hover:border-am-red transition-all group"
            >
              {post.featuredImage && (
                <img
                  src={post.featuredImage}
                  alt={post.featuredImageAlt}
                  class="w-full aspect-video object-cover block border-b-4 border-am-dark"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              )}
              <div class="p-8">
                <p class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-am-dark mb-2">
                  {new Date(post.date).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
                    year: 'numeric',
                    month: 'long',
                    day: 'numeric'
                  })} • {post.readTime} • By {post.author}
                </p>
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-am-dark mb-3 group-hover:text-am-red transition-colors">
                  {post.title}
                </h2>
                <p class="font-bold text-am-dark text-lg mb-4">{post.description}</p>
                <span class="btn-am btn-am-primary inline-block">Read More →</span>
              </div>
            </a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      {sortedPosts.length === 0 && (
        <div class="text-center py-12">
          <p class="text-xl font-bold text-am-dark">Coming soon!</p>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-600">Subscribe to stay updated.</p>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Newsletter CTA */}
  <section class="bg-am-blue border-b-4 border-am-dark py-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4">
      <NewsletterSignup
        client:load
        variant="card"
        source="blog"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Latest Post Featured */}
  {sortedPosts.length > 0 && (
    <section class="bg-am-white border-b-4 border-am-dark py-20">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-am-dark mb-8">Latest Update</h2>
        <a href={`/blog/${sortedPosts[0].slug}`} class="border-4 border-am-dark block hover:shadow-am-lg hover:border-am-red transition-all group">
          {sortedPosts[0].featuredImage && (
            <img
              src={sortedPosts[0].featuredImage}
              alt={sortedPosts[0].featuredImageAlt}
              class="w-full aspect-video object-cover block border-b-4 border-am-dark"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          )}
          <div class="p-8 bg-am-light">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-am-dark mb-4 group-hover:text-am-red transition-colors">
              {sortedPosts[0].title}
            </h3>
            <p class="font-bold text-am-dark mb-6">{sortedPosts[0].description}</p>
            <span class="btn-am btn-am-primary inline-block">Read Full Post →</span>
          </div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}
</Layout>
